  "articleBody": "When you're framing for a new window, it's easy to make a few common blunders that can cause real headaches down the line. Let's talk about some of these and how to steer clear of them. One of the biggest mistakes is using the exact window size for your rough opening instead of consulting the manufacturer’s required dimensions. Manufacturers always specify a rough opening that's a bit larger than the window itself to allow for shimming, insulation, and proper sealing. If you go too tight, you won't have room for adjustment, and your window might not sit correctly or seal properly, leading to drafts or leaks. Another frequent error is failing to measure the opening in multiple places. Walls aren't always perfectly straight or consistent, so checking the width, height, and diagonals at several points ensures you catch any variations before they become a problem. An opening that's even slightly off square can make window installation a nightmare. Ending up with an opening that's either too tight or too wide for shimming and sealing is a recipe for frustration. You need that precise amount of space to ensure a snug, weather-tight fit. If you're dealing with a load-bearing wall, unsure about header sizing, or if the rough opening needs to be corrected after demolition, that’s definitely a time to call in a professional. Getting expert help from HH Remodeling LLC can prevent these mistakes and ensure your window framing is done right the first time. Reach out to us at 303-505-0866 for peace of mind.",
  "datePublished": "2026-08-09"
